# Windows batch files must keep CRLF line endings on checkout, on every
# platform. cmd.exe reads a .bat in the OEM codepage and mis-parses bare LF:
# the `rem` lines stop being comments and it tries to run them as commands.
# Without this, a clone on Linux/macOS — or a repo with core.autocrlf=input —
# stages LF and `run.bat` prints a screenful of "is not recognized" before it
# ever reaches Python. Matches the same rule in FreeClaw's own .gitattributes.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell scripts keep CRLF too, and stay pure ASCII — bootstrap.ps1 is run
# with -File rather than piped through `irm | iex`, but a BOM or a stray
# non-ASCII byte is still a parse hazard worth ruling out the same way
# FreeClaw's install.ps1 does.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Everything else (Python, Markdown) is LF, including on a Windows checkout.
# CPython doesn't care either way, but a mixed-ending diff is a bad one to
# review.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
